Specifically, the "summon nasties" monster spell will try to
summon monsters from the list of /nasties/ (*not* the monsters
considered "nasty"; that's entirely different -- there should
be a clearer way of distinguishing these, but AFAIK there
unfortunately isn't); if this does not succeed (as the monster
is genocided, in this case), it will instead create a random monster.

This random monster, unlike the normally created nasties, doesn't
have to be of a lower level than the caster in case of it
being a spellcaster. On the positive side, random monster selection
should respect extinction. (Of course, if you're going to extinct
Archons anyway, that's going to be either quite boring or really
exciting (in a bad way).)
On the other hand, random monster selection -- unlike the list
of nasties -- may generate Archons and other ungenocidable
summoners that will proceed to enhance your playing experience.

It's actually somewhat unlikely to get an Archonstorm that way,
AFAIK -- that is, unless you actually genocide much more than
people usually do. Archons aren't generated *that* often, so
chain summoning seems unlikely unless you already have many
summoners present (which is a problem on its own, typically).


